#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198058 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a77db107-d157-5063-b44a-1ca0a93be099" :question/math-academy-id "e-12951" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$\n2. $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthogonal\n3. $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$.\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n- $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\mid \\mid = \\mid \\mid \\mathbf{x} \\mid \\mid$.\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$. In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ since\n$\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)\\cdot\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2) = \\mathbf{u}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ are unit vectors:\n$‖Q(u_{1})‖|= ‖u_{1}‖ = \\sqrt{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}} = 1; ‖Q(u_{2})‖|= ‖u_{2}‖ = \\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}} = 1$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198059 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "0070a453-0237-571f-84fc-a9f58446e6a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12954" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. If $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$, find\n\n1. $\\Vert \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\Vert$.\n2. $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$.\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n- $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\mid \\mid = \\mid \\mid \\mathbf{x} \\mid \\mid$.\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$. In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Q(x)‖ &= ‖x‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) \\cdot Q(y) &= x \\cdot y \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot 3 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a547e1a4-d60f-54d2-bfa1-b8c079f0e135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194972} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0688bf-9fba-5a14-aa8f-5a929230a548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194973} {:answer/id #uuid "fe92de50-a599-5150-b03f-431b2e9f8dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194974} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea6f7b5-64dc-56b2-9270-7137b85133b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194975} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa4b776-782b-54ca-b7b7-0b7eabbbcb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea6f7b5-64dc-56b2-9270-7137b85133b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194975} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0c085b-de4b-557c-a42f-dd591367aaf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194971}] :question/id #uuid "70a4471c-4be9-5243-a617-e32ac5259d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-177075" :question/problem "$u_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, u_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u_{1} \\cdot u_{2} = 0$\n2. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n3. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthonormal"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1bc02a8-bd65-5e32-b001-c5a0e88c5c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194978} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9f1ffc-4bc9-5d3d-ae69-4fd9b052c568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194979} {:answer/id #uuid "0390f97d-a40e-5b13-a3db-26e116f1ef7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194980} {:answer/id #uuid "39b143d8-f022-57fe-8701-1dfc1ea0d1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194981} {:answer/id #uuid "6567dd61-915f-584a-8bd9-49056210d9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39b143d8-f022-57fe-8701-1dfc1ea0d1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194981} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc782e18-1be0-5c27-8a37-7f4bb7695511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194977}] :question/id #uuid "e6d806a7-8383-51f7-a460-be3b003d424b" :question/math-academy-id "q-177315" :question/problem "$u_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, u_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u_{1} \\cdot u_{2} = 0$\n2. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n3. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthonormal"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd541855-878e-56f3-a786-7a57370ed439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197672} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ea6a3-4067-5735-b2fd-a636a1e85139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197673} {:answer/id #uuid "1397c106-4552-50b3-9c35-572dcb670a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197674} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfd28d1-c4f1-5883-97d4-cd349ee2fd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197675} {:answer/id #uuid "47de0aa1-72d1-5565-9786-240e35eb9043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1397c106-4552-50b3-9c35-572dcb670a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197674} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e4a6322-a6d8-56a1-9b9c-113d2d233973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197671}] :question/id #uuid "063b2ff3-4387-5a0a-88b2-8a1c68641ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40516" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x) \\cdot Q(y)$, where $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$."}]}
